{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:rstar:redteam","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:0bd9cdd42e725f8246760cdf2882efa1d4017b74802b525343a1f85c78c46849","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.2,"B":0.51,"C":0.19,"D":0.1}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"LEXICAL PRIORITY applied as written, first match wins, on my own CQ3 growth measure and GDP-weighted OECD-38 core over 2032-2036 (NOT CQ2's 2027-2036 window; I add ~+0.15pp for the back half, since the care/grid bottleneck and the debt-service claim both load later).\nSTEP 1 — growth bands from MY CQ3 (P10 1.05 / P50 2.00 / P90 3.05, sigma ~0.78, slight left skew): P(g>=2.6) = 0.20; P(2.0<=g<2.6) = 0.30; P(1.7<=g<2.0) = 0.15; P(g<1.7) = 0.35.\nSTEP 2 — inflation. P(OECD-38 GDP-weighted core 2032-36 >= 2.7%) = 0.34, Turkiye-inclusive (~0.25 ex-Turkiye; that ambiguity over which published aggregate resolves lands almost entirely on cell C). P(core < 2.0%) = 0.17 — the number that most separates me from Wave 1. A GDP-weighted OECD-38 core below 2.0% WITH Turkiye in the basket requires the ex-Turkiye core at roughly 1.6%, a hard bar; P3 saw this and priced it at 0.26 conditional, while P1 used 0.38 unconditional and P2 0.25-0.40, both built from US/euro/Japan blocs only. Turkiye's inclusion suppresses D and inflates C, and it is the largest single index-construction transfer in this question.\nSTEP 3 — lexical allocation. g>=2.6 -> A entirely = 0.200. 2.0<=g<2.6 -> always B (rule 2 needs g<2.0, rule 3 needs g<1.7) = 0.300. 1.7<=g<2.0 -> C if core>=2.7 (conditional 0.40, positive dependence because the supply wall that raises the price caps the quantity) = 0.060, else B = 0.090. g<1.7 -> conditional split 0.38 core>=2.7 / 0.26 core<2.0 / 0.36 in between: C += 0.133, D = 0.091, B += 0.126. TOTALS: A 0.200, B 0.516, C 0.193, D 0.091 -> 20 / 51 / 19 / 10, summing to exactly 100.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ3: A is identically P(g>=2.6) = 20 by construction, since rule 1 decides A on growth alone. My P50 of 2.00% sits at the boundary of B's literal band, so mass is concentrated in B+C+D (80) and emphatically not in A — I have not violated the lock's stated example. P(g<2.0) from my CQ3 = 0.50, and the table's low-growth mass is C 19 + D 10 + the low-growth slice of B (0.090 + 0.126 = 0.216) = 0.506. Closes.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ2: my CQ2 conjunct A (core >= 2.50%, 2027-2036) = 0.50. This table implies P(core 2032-36 >= 2.7%) = C's 0.193 plus the core>=2.7 cells that lexically land in A or B (0.200 x 0.26 + 0.300 x 0.30 = 0.142) = 0.335, matching the 0.34 I assumed, and coherent with 0.50 at the looser 2.50% threshold over the longer window. C (19) must sit below 0.34 because it is intersected with g<2.0 — holds. D (10) must sit below P(core<2.0) = 0.17 because it is intersected with g<1.7 — holds. CQ2's joint of 34 exceeds C's 19, as required, since CQ2's OECD leg is weaker (2.50 vs 2.70) but adds a China condition.\nWHERE I ACTUALLY DEPART FROM WAVE 1, since B at 51 happens to land on their centre. P3's residual audit is correct and I adopt it: B is a drafting residual, not a forecast, and ~18pp of its mass comes from cells with no literal home (principally g<1.7 with core 2.0-2.6, the stagnation-without-disinflation cell a naive reader assigns to D). The ensemble should not read B = 51 as 51% confidence in the Bifurcated Barbell narrative. My real disagreements are A at 20 against their 10-11, and D at 10 against their 16-19. A is higher because I refuse to treat 2A's \"absent productivity offsets\" counterfactual as a growth baseline, and because capital deepening in a shrinking workforce is close to arithmetic. D is the cell my CQ4 thesis most strongly excludes: D requires low growth AND core under 2.0%, i.e. the demand-shortfall world — but that is the world in which saving is abundant, the sovereign clears cheaply and real yields are LOW. I cannot hold a 2.45% G7 real-yield P50 and simultaneously put 19 points on the excess-saving regime. My CQ4 and my CQ6-D are the same bet and I price them consistently. Finally, the lock makes A's prose clauses (\"broad wage gains\", \"debt stabilizes via growth\") NON-OPERATIVE — A resolves on growth alone — so my CQ1 of 14 (broad wage gains very unlikely) and my A of 20 are not in conflict; that is a lock artifact, flagged rather than hedged against.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:53.625Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"The New York Fed's HLW r* estimate for the United States (or the Richmond Fed's Lubik-Matthes companion) is published at or below 1.0% for the 2027 and 2028 vintages, with no upward revision to the 2024-26 history.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The cleanest direct refutation of my central claim that the filter is revising upward into a regime break. CQ4 P50 falls from 2.45% toward 1.95-2.05% (Wave-1 territory), my variance attribution flips back to premium-dominant, CQ5 falls ~5pp to roughly 25 as the MOVE level channel weakens, and CQ6-D rises from 10 toward 15 at B's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The US 10y TIPS real yield (FRED DFII10) averages below 1.70% over any rolling twelve months, in the ABSENCE of an announced yield-curve-control, duration-shortening or captive-buyer mandate from the Federal Reserve or Treasury.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Mean reversion down without a repression mechanism is exactly the move Wave 1 asserted and I said had no mechanism. CQ4 P50 down ~50bp to ~1.95% and P90 down to ~3.2; CQ6-D up ~6pp and CQ6-A down ~4pp, since cheap duration signals abundant saving. If it instead happens WITH an 